grammar => [
['Imperatives in your own head, declaratives to the room', q{Your notes can be imperative — <em>"search the title, read three profiles"</em> — but interview answers must be declarative and past-tense: <em>"I read three PO profiles and noticed all of them mention mobile app 7.0."</em> Practice: convert every research note into one past-simple sentence you could say aloud. Notes are for you; sentences are for them.}, q{给自己看的笔记可以用祈使句（"search the title, read three profiles"），但面试回答必须是陈述句和过去时："I read three PO profiles and noticed…"。练习方法：把每条调研笔记改写成一句可以脱口而出的过去时句子。笔记给自己，句子给对方。}],
['Because-clauses prove inference', q{<em>"Both mean the digital channels team is a cost-and-growth centre."</em> The grammar that makes research impressive is <strong>fact + because-clause + implication</strong>: "They are shrinking branches, <strong>which means</strong>…", "The ad asks for roadmap ownership, <strong>because</strong> login is where customers leak." Anyone can quote a fact; the which-means clause is what turns you from a reader into an analyst.}, q{让调研显得有深度的语法是<strong>事实 + 因果从句 + 推论</strong>："They are shrinking branches, which means…"、"The ad asks for roadmap ownership, because login is where customers leak."。引用事实人人都会；which means 从句才把读者变成分析者。}],
],
tips => [
q{<strong>Two facts said well beat twenty facts said badly.</strong> Prepare two strategy sentences and two org sentences. No more.},
q{<strong>Use their words.</strong> If the annual report says "digitise at scale", you say "digitise at scale" — not your paraphrase of it.},
q{<strong>App-store reviews are free user research.</strong> Three one-star reviews tell you the team\'s real backlog better than any press release.},
q{<strong>Do not quote confidential internals.</strong> If a friend tells you something non-public, keep it as context, never as an answer — it signals poor judgement.},
q{<strong>Your reverse questions are the exam\'s second half.</strong> Prepare exactly three: programme, team, success criteria.},
],
