grammar => [
['Past perfect for the pre-alignment timeline', q{<em>"I pre-aligned his deputy before the meeting, so he walked in with an ally."</em> The sequencing words (before, pre-) plus past simple keep the chain readable when events compress. If narrating a longer chain, escalate to past perfect: <em>"By the time the meeting started, I had already shared the memo with his deputy."</em> — one had-done for the earliest action orients the listener instantly.}, q{时间顺序词（before、pre-）+ 一般过去时让压缩的事件链保持可读。叙述更长的链条时升级为过去完成时："By the time the meeting started, I had already shared the memo with his deputy."——用一句 had done 标出最早的动作，听者瞬间定位。}],
['Contrast pairs carry judgement', q{<em>"Alignment is politics only when the information differs."</em>, <em>"Being right is table stakes, being trusted is the job."</em> The grammar is a contrast pair: [X is Y] only when [Z] / [A is table stakes], [B is the job]. These balanced sentences are how professionals encode judgement memorably — two clauses, one pivot, no hedging. Build three of these per interview.}, q{对比句是职业判断的记忆点：[X is Y] only when [Z] / [A is table stakes], [B is the job]。两个分句、一个转折、不含糊。每次面试准备三句这样的平衡句。}],
],
tips => [
q{<strong>Say the STAR labels in panels.</strong> Three note-takers need your skeleton; making it audible raises every interviewer\'s score of "structured".},
q{<strong>The transcript test answers ethics probes.</strong> Tomas\'s "influence or politics?" question has a clean grammar: state the test, apply it, stop.},
q{<strong>Prepare the bad version of your best story.</strong> "When has this gone badly?" is a guaranteed follow-up — your failure story should be the prequel of your good one.},
q{<strong>Keep numbers honest and approximate.</strong> "Roughly 40 minutes, down from about 4 hours" survives scrutiny; invented precision does not.},
q{<strong>Watch the chair, answer the room.</strong> Grace asked; Anita and Tomas scored. Glance at all three — panels hire people who can hold a room.},
],
practice => [
{ t => 'Door selection drill', p => 'Your story: "the deadline I saved". Which door does it open for each question: (a) tell me about pressure, (b) tell me about prioritisation, (c) tell me about a conflict?', a => q{All three, with different emphasis: (a) spotlight the 2 AM triage; (b) spotlight what you cut and why; (c) spotlight the negotiation with whoever demanded the impossible date. One story, three spotlights — write the spotlights on the card.} },
{ t => 'The follow-up you fear', p => 'Your strongest story ends with a win. Draft your answer to: "What would you do differently?" in two sentences — one specific change, one mechanism you now use.', a => q{Weak: "Nothing major, it went well." Strong: "I would write the memo on day one, not day five — written options now start every disagreement I am in." Specific + mechanism = growth the panel can verify.} },
{ t => 'Contrast-pair factory', p => 'Write three contrast pairs you could use as lessons: [being X is …], [being Y is …]. Bank-quality examples: "delivering is table stakes, deciding what to deliver is the job".', a => q{Your three should be about your craft, not generic wisdom. Bad: "work hard, play hard." Good: "shipping is table stakes, knowing what not to ship is the job" — a Tech Lead lesson in one line.} },
],
},
{
num => 6, slug => 'explaining-a-technical-project', module => 2, read => 8, diff => '🟡 Intermediate',
title => 'Explaining a Technical Project', cn => '讲清一个技术项目',
focus => 'The two-layer project pitch: business story first, depth on demand',
scenario => [
q{Technical interview, question three: <em>"Pick a project on your CV and walk me through it."</em> You pick the one you know best — a <strong>payment reconciliation engine</strong> you built with two colleagues. The interviewer, <strong>Ruth</strong>, is a principal engineer. She will test one thing: <strong>can you size your explanation to your audience?</strong>},
q{技术面试第三问：<em>"挑一个简历上的项目讲一讲。"</em>你选了最熟的一个——你和两位同事一起做的<strong>支付对账引擎</strong>。面试官 <strong>Ruth</strong> 是首席工程师。她要验证的只有一件事：<strong>你能否根据听众调整讲解的深度？</strong>},
q{The trap is depth-first. Candidates who start with schema diagrams lose the interviewer before the story starts. The professional shape is <strong>two layers</strong>: a 60-second business layer anyone can follow, then an invitation — <em>"want me to go deeper on the design?"</em> — that lets the interviewer pick the depth.},
q{陷阱是"深度优先"。从表结构图开讲的候选人还没进故事就把面试官讲丢了。职业化的结构是<strong>两层</strong>：先讲 60 秒任何人都能懂的业务层，然后给一个邀请——<em>"需要我深入讲设计吗？"</em>——把深度选择权交给面试官。},
],
dialogue => [
['Ruth (Principal Engineer)', q{Pick a project from your CV and walk me through it — <em>start wherever makes sense to you</em>.}, q{从简历里挑一个项目讲讲——<em>从你认为合理的地方开始</em>。}],
['You (Candidate)', q{I\'ll do the <strong>payment reconciliation engine</strong>. <strong>The one-sentence version:</strong> our bank moved about two million transactions a day between three systems, and roughly 900 of them failed to match every night — each one a manual investigation.}, q{我讲<strong>支付对账引擎</strong>。<strong>一句话版本：</strong>我们银行每天在三个系统间流动约两百万笔交易，每晚大约 900 笔对不平——每一笔都要人工排查。}],
['You (Candidate)', q{<strong>The business problem:</strong> those 900 mismatches cost the operations team about 60 person-hours a week, and the backlog grew faster than the team. <strong>We automated the matching, cut the daily mismatches to under 40, and gave the remaining ones a triage UI.</strong>}, q{<strong>业务问题：</strong>这 900 笔对不平每周消耗运营团队约 60 个人时，且积压增长快于人手。<strong>我们自动化了匹配，把每日不平降到 40 笔以下，并给剩余的做了分诊界面。</strong>}],
['You (Candidate)', q{<strong>My part:</strong> I designed the matching pipeline and the exception model. My two colleagues built the UI and the reconciliation rules engine. <em>Want me to go deeper on the matching design, the rollout, or the failure modes?</em>}, q{<strong>我的部分：</strong>匹配管道和异常模型的设计。两位同事负责界面和对账规则引擎。<em>要我深入讲匹配设计、上线过程，还是故障模式？</em>}],
['Ruth (Principal Engineer)', q{Matching design. <em>And don\'t spare the detail.</em>}, q{匹配设计。<em>细节不用省。</em>}],
['You (Candidate)', q{<strong>The core insight:</strong> 98% of mismatches are not mysterious — they are ordering and timing. So the pipeline runs in passes. <strong>Pass one:</strong> exact match on ID, amount, currency — clears 90% in minutes. <strong>Pass two:</strong> fuzzy match within a tolerance window — amount within a cent, timestamp within ten minutes. That clears most of the rest.}, q{<strong>核心洞察：</strong>98% 的不平并不神秘——只是顺序和时间差。所以管道分多轮跑。<strong>第一轮：</strong>ID、金额、币种精确匹配——几分钟内清掉 90%。<strong>第二轮：</strong>容差窗口内模糊匹配——金额差一分钱内、时间差十分钟内。又清掉大部分。}],
['You (Candidate)', q{<strong>Pass three is the part I am proud of:</strong> instead of more rules, the leftovers get a <em>feature vector</em> — counterparty, hour-of-day, failure history — and we rank them by likelihood of being the same transaction. The ops team works the ranked list, top first. <strong>Human attention became the scarce resource we optimised for.</strong>}, q{<strong>第三轮是我最自豪的：</strong>剩余的不再堆规则，而是生成<em>特征向量</em>——对手方、时段、失败历史——按"是同一笔交易的可能性"排序。运营团队按排名处理，从最像的开始。<strong>人的注意力成了我们优化的稀缺资源。</strong>}],
['Ruth (Principal Engineer)', q{What broke? <em>Tell me about a failure mode you did not expect.</em>}, q{出了什么问题？<em>讲一个你没预料到的故障模式。</em>}],
['You (Candidate)', q{Time zones. <strong>Pass two\'s tolerance window</strong> silently matched transactions across a daylight-saving boundary — same cent, ten minutes apart, wrong pair. It produced 12 false matches before we caught it. <strong>The fix taught me more than the build:</strong> every tolerance now carries the calendar it was computed under, and the audit log can replay any match decision.}, q{时区。<strong>第二轮的容差窗口</strong>在夏令时边界上静默配对了交易——金额一致、时间差十分钟、但配错了。发现前产生了 12 笔错误匹配。<strong>修复比建设教得更多：</strong>现在每个容差都带着计算它所用的日历，审计日志可以回放任何一次匹配决定。}],
['Ruth (Principal Engineer)', q{Good depth, and good honesty. Last thing — <em>what would you build differently today?</em>}, q{深度不错，态度也诚实。最后一个问题——<em>今天重做你会怎么改？</em>}],
['You (Candidate)', q{Two things. I would <strong>start with the audit log, not bolt it on later</strong> — replay turned out to be the feature ops actually loved. And I would question pass two earlier: we shipped it on day one because it was easy, <em>but "easy to build" is not a reason, it is just a fact about building</em>.}, q{两点。<strong>审计日志会从第一天就做，而不是后来补</strong>——回放才是运营真正爱的功能。另外我会更早质疑第二轮：第一天就上线它只因为它简单，<em>但"容易实现"不是理由，只是关于实现的事实</em>。}],
],
phrases => [
['Layer', q{The one-sentence version: …}, q{一句话版本：……}, q{Earns the right to continue. If the one sentence lands, everything after it is listened to.}],
['Business', q{Cost the operations team about 60 person-hours a week.}, q{每周消耗运营团队约 60 个人时。}, q{Translate tech into person-hours and money — this is the language that gets projects funded.}],
['Offer', q{Deeper on the design, the rollout, or the failure modes?}, q{深入讲设计、上线，还是故障模式？}, q{The menu question — hands depth control to the interviewer and shows you know there are layers.}],
['Insight', q{98% of mismatches are not mysterious — they are ordering and timing.}, q{98% 的不平并不神秘——只是顺序和时间差。}, q{One insight sentence before the mechanics makes all mechanics feel purposeful.}],
['Ownership', q{My part: I designed… My colleagues built…}, q{我的部分：我设计……同事负责……}, q{Credit-sharing that still draws a precise line around your contribution.}],
['War story', q{The fix taught me more than the build.}, q{修复比建设教得更多。}, q{Failure modes asked and answered with a lesson — principal engineers score this heavily.}],
['Growth', q{"Easy to build" is not a reason, it is just a fact about building.}, q{"容易实现"不是理由，只是关于实现的事实。}, q{A portable engineering aphorism — ending on one marks seniority.}],
],
vocab => [
['Reconciliation', 'n. 对账', 'Matching records across systems to confirm they agree.'],
['Mismatch', 'n. 对不平', 'A record pair that should match but does not.'],
['Triage', 'n. 分诊', 'Sorting items by priority so attention goes to the worst first.'],
['Pass', 'n. 轮（处理轮次）', 'One sweep of matching logic over the data; pipelines run in passes.'],
['Tolerance window', 'n. 容差窗口', 'The allowed difference (amount, time) within which records may match.'],
['Feature vector', 'n. 特征向量', 'A set of attributes describing an item for ranking or matching.'],
['False match', 'n. 错误配对', 'Two records paired that are not actually the same — worse than none.'],
['Daylight-saving boundary', 'n. 夏令时边界', 'Clock-change dates that break time-based logic. Classic.'],
['Audit log', 'n. 审计日志', 'The record of who did what when — in banks, a first-class feature.'],
['Rollout', 'n. 上线推广', 'The staged release of a system into production.'],
],
grammar => [
['So + result clause sells the design', q{<em>"So the pipeline runs in passes."</em>, <em>"so this stopped being…"</em>, <em>"so I came prepared…"</em> — <strong>so</strong> is the interview engineer\'s favourite connective: [insight], so [design]. It makes every mechanical choice feel derived rather than memorised: "98% are ordering and timing, so the pipeline runs in passes." Say the insight first, then the so-clause, and the design explains itself.}, q{<strong>so</strong> 是面试工程师最爱的连接词：[洞察]，so [设计]。"98% 是顺序和时间差，so the pipeline runs in passes." 让每个机械选择都显得是推导出来的而非背出来的。先说洞察，再说 so 从句，设计自会说明自己。}],
['Cleft sentences spotlight the proud part', q{<em>"Pass three is the part I am proud of."</em>, <em>"replay turned out to be the feature ops actually loved."</em> — the <strong>[X] is the [thing] that…</strong> pattern points the interviewer\'s attention deliberately. Use one per project: mark the component you want questions about. Interviewers follow spotlights; give them yours.}, q{"Pass three is the part I am proud of."、'replay turned out to be the feature ops actually loved.'——<strong>[X] is the [thing] that…</strong> 句式刻意引导面试官的注意力。每个项目用一次：标出你希望被追问的组件。面试官会跟随聚光灯；把灯打在你想要的地方。}],
],
tips => [
q{<strong>Business layer first, always.</strong> 60 seconds: what it was, what it cost, what changed. Diagrams come after the invitation.},
q{<strong>Offer a menu, not a monologue.</strong> "Deeper on design, rollout, or failure modes?" respects the interviewer\'s time and scores communication.},
q{<strong>Have the war story ready.</strong> "What broke?" is a certainty in technical interviews. The daylight-saving bug is a better answer than "nothing major".},
q{<strong>Claim your slice precisely.</strong> "I designed the pipeline; my colleagues built the UI" is honest and strong. Blurry "we" answers invite suspicion.},
q{<strong>End on a grown-up aphorism.</strong> "Easy to build is not a reason" — one sentence that says you reflect by default.},
],
practice => [
{ t => 'Two-layer your own project', p => 'Take your best project. Write the one-sentence version, the 60-second business layer (problem, cost, outcome with a number), and a three-item depth menu.', a => q{Test: could a non-engineer follow the business layer? Does the menu name the three parts you could talk about for ten minutes each? If the menu is empty, you are not ready to present this project.} },
{ t => 'Cost translation', p => 'Translate these into business language: (a) "p95 latency 400ms", (b) "30% test coverage gap", (c) "manual deployment takes 2 engineers 4 hours".', a => q{(a) "checkout feels slow enough that we lose conversions at peak"; (b) "changes ship with avoidable regressions that hit production monthly"; (c) "every release burns 8 person-hours and takes a change-freeze window". Numbers become hours, money, risk.} },
{ t => 'War-story slot', p => 'For your project, write the three-sentence answer to "what broke?" — bug, impact, and the lesson that changed how you build.', a => q{Structure: "Time zones silently matched across the DST boundary — 12 false matches before we caught it. Every tolerance now carries its calendar, and any decision can be replayed. The fix taught me more than the build." Bug in one line, mechanism in one line, growth in one line.} },
],
},
{
num => 7, slug => 'design-talk-through', module => 2, read => 9, diff => '🔴 Advanced',
title => 'System Design Talk-Through', cn => '系统设计题讲解',
focus => 'Think aloud, drive with questions, trade off honestly — design interviews are dialogue',
scenario => [
q{Final round for a <em>Senior Developer</em> role: the design exercise. <strong>Mei</strong>, an architect, slides a one-line prompt across the table: <em>"Design a notification service for a retail bank — email, push, SMS, 20 million customers."</em> Then she says the sentence that defines the next 40 minutes: <strong>"I care less about the destination and more about the journey — think aloud."</strong>},
q{<em>Senior Developer</em> 岗位的最终轮：设计题。<strong>Mei</strong>（架构师）把一行题目推到桌面：<em>"为一个零售银行设计通知服务——邮件、推送、短信，2000 万客户。"</em>然后她说了一句定义接下来 40 分钟的话：<strong>"我更在乎过程而不是终点——把思考说出来。"</strong>},
q{A design interview is <strong>not a whiteboard exam, it is a dialogue</strong>. The candidates who fail are not the ones with wrong answers — they are the ones who go silent, or who never ask what the problem actually is. Your tools: clarifying questions, visible trade-offs, and a steady stream of thinking-aloud.},
q{设计面试<strong>不是白板考试，而是对话</strong>。失败的候选人不是答案错的——而是陷入沉默、或从不追问问题本质的人。你的工具：澄清问题、外显的取舍、以及源源不断的思考外化。},
],
dialogue => [
['Mei (Architect)', q{Design a notification service for a retail bank — email, push, SMS, 20 million customers. <em>Think aloud; I care about the journey.</em>}, q{为零售银行设计通知服务——邮件、推送、短信，2000 万客户。<em>把思考说出来；我看重过程。</em>}],
['You (Candidate)', q{Before drawing anything — <strong>two clarifying questions</strong>. First: are notifications <em>marketing or operational</em>? A fraud alert and a credit-card ad have very different reliability contracts. Second: what does <strong>"late" mean here</strong> — is a balance alert 5 minutes late a bug or fine?}, q{画图之前——<strong>两个澄清问题</strong>。第一：通知是<em>营销还是业务运营</em>？反欺诈提醒和信用卡广告的可靠性契约完全不同。第二：这里<strong>"迟到"意味着什么</strong>——余额提醒晚 5 分钟是故障还是可接受？}],
['Mei (Architect)', q{Operational only — fraud, balance, service alerts. And late is a bug over one minute for fraud, ten for the rest.}, q{只做业务运营——反欺诈、余额、服务提醒。反欺诈迟到 1 分钟算故障，其他 10 分钟。}],
['You (Candidate)', q{That shapes everything. <strong>Fraud alerts are the critical path</strong>, so they get priority lanes and tighter SLAs; the rest can batch. Let me sketch the shape: producers publish events, a router maps event types to channels, a per-channel sender fleet, and — because this is a bank — <strong>a full audit trail and idempotent sends</strong>.}, q{这决定了整个设计。<strong>反欺诈提醒是关键路径</strong>，享有优先通道和更严的 SLA；其余可以批量。画一下整体：生产方发布事件，路由器把事件类型映射到渠道，每个渠道一个发送集群，而且——这是银行——<strong>完整审计链路 + 幂等发送</strong>。}],
['Mei (Architect)', q{Why idempotent? <em>Convince me.</em>}, q{为什么要幂等？<em>说服我。</em>}],
['You (Candidate)', q{Because retries are not failures in this system — they are Tuesday. <strong>Every sender will time out and retry</strong>, and a fraud alert sent twice is not redundancy, it is a trust problem with the customer. So every notification carries an idempotency key; the senders treat duplicates as no-ops. <em>Better to dedupe inside the service than to apologise outside it.</em>}, q{因为在这个系统里重试不是故障——是家常便饭。<strong>每个发送方都会超时重试</strong>，反欺诈提醒发两次不是冗余，是客户信任问题。所以每条通知带幂等键，发送方把重复当作无操作。<em>宁可服务内去重，不要服务外道歉。</em>}],
['Mei (Architect)', q{20 million customers, fraud alert at midnight. <em>Where does this break first?</em>}, q{2000 万客户，午夜反欺诈提醒。<em>哪里先坏？</em>}],
['You (Candidate)', q{Honest answer: <strong>the SMS gateway is a third party and I do not own it</strong> — my first bottleneck is probably their throughput, which is why the design needs a queue with backpressure rather than a direct call. Second break: <strong>the fan-out</strong> — 20 million rows is fine to store, but building 20 million personalised messages at midnight needs pre-computed templates, not string-building at send time.}, q{诚实回答：<strong>短信网关是第三方，不归我管</strong>——第一个瓶颈很可能是他们的吞吐量，所以设计上要排队 + 背压，而不是直接调用。第二个坏点：<strong>扇出</strong>——2000 万行存储没问题，但午夜生成 2000 万条个性化消息需要预计算模板，而不是发送时拼字符串。}],
['Mei (Architect)', q{Good. <em>What did you choose NOT to build, and why?</em>}, q{好。<em>你选择不做什么？为什么？</em>}],
['You (Candidate)', q{Three deliberate noes. <strong>No user-facing preferences UI</strong> — out of scope today, but the schema reserves the flags. <strong>No custom SMS infrastructure</strong> — a commodity third party is cheaper than being special. <strong>No real-time analytics</strong> — valuable, but not before the reliability contract is proven. <em>Every one of those is a one-way door I would rather leave open than walk through early.</em>}, q{三个刻意的"不"。<strong>不做用户偏好界面</strong>——今天超纲，但表结构预留字段。<strong>不自建短信基础设施</strong>——标准化第三方比自建便宜。<strong>不做实时分析</strong>——有价值，但排在可靠性契约被验证之后。<em>每一项都是我宁可留着不走的单向门。</em>}],
['Mei (Architect)', q{That is the answer I was listening for. Well done.}, q{这正是我在等的答案。表现出色。}],
],
phrases => [
['Clarify', q{Before drawing anything — two clarifying questions.}, q{画图之前——先问两个澄清问题。}, q{The strongest opening move in a design interview. Requirements are the first test.}],
['Think aloud', q{That shapes everything.}, q{这一下决定了整个设计。}, q{Says out loud that new information changed the design — the exact skill being scored.}],
['Prioritise', q{Fraud alerts are the critical path.}, q{反欺诈提醒是关键路径。}, q{Name the critical path explicitly; it licenses every later prioritisation.}],
