grammar => [
['Conditionals frame the break points', q{<em>"If 20 million fraud alerts queue at midnight, the SMS gateway saturates first."</em> Design interviews run on first conditionals — [if scenario], [consequence]. The structure keeps your speculation disciplined: every hypothetical has a named consequence, which is what separates analysis from guessing. Keep the if-clause concrete: numbers, times, components.}, q{设计面试靠第一类条件句运转：[if 场景]，[后果]。"If 20 million fraud alerts queue at midnight, the SMS gateway saturates first." 这个结构让推测保持纪律：每个假设都有明确的后果——这是分析与瞎猜的分界。if 从句要具体：数字、时间、组件。}],
['Gerunds name the patterns', q{<em>"Building 20 million personalised messages at midnight needs pre-computed templates."</em> — <strong>building</strong> as subject turns an activity into a designable thing. The same grammar carries your noes: <em>"No custom SMS infrastructure — being special is expensive."</em> Gerunds let you talk about kinds of work (fan-out, dedupe, string-building) as objects you can trade off.}, q{<strong>building</strong> 作主语把活动变成可设计的对象："Building 20 million personalised messages at midnight needs pre-computed templates." 同样的语法支撑你的"不"："No custom SMS infrastructure — being special is expensive." 动名词让你把一类工作（扇出、去重、拼字符串）当作可权衡的对象来谈。}],
],
tips => [
q{<strong>Clarify before you draw.</strong> Two questions in the first two minutes signal seniority louder than any architecture.},
q{<strong>Say what changed your mind.</strong> "That shapes everything" is thinking aloud in its purest form — interviewers cannot read your head; narrate it.},
q{<strong>Name what you do not own.</strong> Third-party gateways, upstream teams, customer behaviour — saying "I do not control that" is system thinking, not weakness.},
q{<strong>Have a "what I did not build" list.</strong> Scope discipline is the most senior signal in design interviews — every no needs a one-line why.},
q{<strong>Silence is the only real failure.</strong> A mediocre architecture discussed honestly outscores a brilliant one delivered mute.},
],
practice => [
{ t => 'Clarify first', p => 'For "design a payment refund service", write the four clarifying questions you would ask before drawing anything.', a => q{Strong set: internal-only or customer-facing refunds? What volume and peak (sales events)? What is the SLA — when does "late" become a bug? What exists already — ledger, payment service, or greenfield? Requirements first; architecture second.} },
{ t => 'Trade-off aphorisms', p => 'Turn these into one-line trade-off statements: (a) dedupe vs duplicate sends, (b) queue vs direct call to a weak dependency, (c) build vs buy for SMS.', a => q{(a) "Better to dedupe inside than apologise outside." (b) "A queue absorbs what a direct call would broadcast." (c) "Be special where you differentiate; be commodity where you don\'t." Each names both sides in one breath.} },
{ t => 'The noes drill', p => 'For the notification service, write the "what I would build differently" answer Mei asked for — two items, each with a mechanism.', a => q{Model: "I would start with the audit log, not bolt it on — replay is the feature ops loves. And I would question the easy pass earlier: easy to build is not a reason." Specific change + portable mechanism, every time.} },
],
},
