title => 'When You Don\'t Know the Answer', cn => '答不上来怎么办',
focus => 'The honest pivot: boundary, method, adjacent knowledge, and the follow-up email',
scenario => [
q{Technical interview, deep in the weeds. <strong>Ruth</strong> asks: <em>"How would you tune the JVM GC for our batch settlement job?"</em> You have never tuned a JVM in your life — you are a .NET developer who just said so two minutes ago. <strong>This moment decides more of your score than any correct answer would.</strong>},
q{技术面试，聊到深处。<strong>Ruth</strong> 问：<em>"你会怎么为我们的批量清算作业调优 JVM GC？"</em>你这辈子没调过 JVM——两分钟前你刚说自己是 .NET 开发。<strong>这个时刻对你分数的影响，超过任何一道答对的题。</strong>},
q{Every interviewer asks something you cannot answer — deliberately. They are not testing knowledge; they are testing <strong>behaviour at the edge of knowledge</strong>: do you bluff, freeze, or work? The professional response has four moves: <strong>name the boundary, show the method, offer the adjacent knowledge, close the loop after.</strong>},
q{每个面试官都会故意问一个你答不上的问题。他们测的不是知识，而是<strong>知识边界上的行为</strong>：你是硬编、僵住，还是开始工作？职业化的回应有四步：<strong>承认边界、展示方法、给出相邻知识、面试后闭环。</strong>},
],
dialogue => [
['Ruth (Principal Engineer)', q{Last technical one — <em>how would you tune the JVM GC for our batch settlement job?</em>}, q{最后一个技术题——<em>你会怎么为批量清算作业调优 JVM GC？</em>}],
['You (Candidate)', q{Straight answer: <strong>I have never tuned a JVM — my GC experience is .NET\'s, and I would not pretend the knobs transfer.</strong> Rather than invent settings, can I tell you <em>how I would get to an answer by Wednesday</em>?}, q{直说：<strong>我没调过 JVM——我的 GC 经验在 .NET 侧，我也不会假装那些旋钮可以平移。</strong>与其编参数，我可以讲讲<em>我怎么在周三前找到答案</em>吗？}],
['Ruth (Principal Engineer)', q{That is the right answer to the question I actually asked. Go on.}, q{这正是我问的真问题的正确答案。请继续。}],
['You (Candidate)', q{Method, four steps. <strong>One:</strong> baseline before knobs — I would capture allocation rate, pause times, and throughput on the current config, or I am tuning blind. <strong>Two:</strong> one variable at a time, in a staging run shaped like the settlement batch — same data volume, same timing. <strong>Three:</strong> read the vendor documentation and the two or three canonical tuning write-ups, because GC folklore is where projects go to die. <strong>Four:</strong> ship the change behind a flag with a rollback plan, and let the batch logs prove or disprove it.}, q{方法四步。<strong>一：</strong>先基线再旋钮——在现有配置下抓分配率、暂停时间、吞吐量，否则是盲调。<strong>二：</strong>一次只动一个变量，在与清算作业同形状的预发环境跑——同样数据量、同样时间窗口。<strong>三：</strong>读厂商文档和两三篇权威调优文章，因为 GC 民间偏方是项目坟场。<strong>四：</strong>改动放在开关后面，带回滚方案，让批处理日志来证明或证伪。}],
['Ruth (Principal Engineer)', q{Good method. <em>What do you know that is adjacent?</em>}, q{方法不错。<em>你有哪些相邻的知识？</em>}],
['You (Candidate)', q{Two things, honestly labelled. <strong>From .NET:</strong> the generational hypothesis transfers — most objects die young, and allocation rate is the real enemy regardless of runtime. <strong>From this kind of job:</strong> batch settlement is allocation-churn-heavy, so my first suspicion would be short-lived object storms in the matching loop, not heap size. <strong>That is a hypothesis, not knowledge — I am marking it as such.</strong>}, q{两块，如实标注。<strong>来自 .NET：</strong>分代假设是通用的——大多数对象朝生夕死，无论哪个运行时，分配率才是真正的敌人。<strong>来自这类作业：</strong>批量清算是分配高扰动场景，所以我首先怀疑的是匹配循环里的短命对象风暴，而不是堆大小。<strong>这是假设，不是知识——我明确标注它是假设。</strong>}],
['Ruth (Principal Engineer)', q{Labeled hypotheses — I like that. <em>Anything you want to add?</em>}, q{标注过的假设——我喜欢。还有想补充的吗？}],
['You (Candidate)', q{One thing: <strong>may I send you a short follow-up after this?</strong> I would spend an evening on the JVM docs and reply with what I would actually try first, and why. <em>Not to change your assessment — to close the loop properly.</em>}, q{一件事：<strong>面试后我可以发你一个简短的跟进吗？</strong>我会花一个晚上读 JVM 文档，回复"我会先试什么、为什么"。<em>不是为了改变你的评价——是把闭环走完。</em>}],
['Ruth (Principal Engineer)', q{Send it. I will read it. Let\'s take five.}, q{发吧，我会看。休息五分钟。}],
],
phrases => [
['Boundary', q{I have never tuned a JVM — and I would not pretend the knobs transfer.}, q{我没调过 JVM——也不会假装那些旋钮可以平移。}, q{The cleanest boundary statement: no apology, no drama, no bluff.}],
['Pivot', q{Can I tell you how I would get to an answer by Wednesday?}, q{我可以讲讲怎么在周三前找到答案吗？}, q{Converts "I don\'t know" into a proposal — the pivot that earns the follow-up.}],
['Method', q{Baseline before knobs.}, q{先基线，再旋钮。}, q{Four-step methods delivered as numbered beats show the working mind, not the memorised fact.}],
['Adjacent', q{The generational hypothesis transfers.}, q{分代假设是通用的。}, q{Adjacent knowledge is real knowledge — but only when the transfer is named, not assumed.}],
['Integrity', q{That is a hypothesis, not knowledge — I am marking it as such.}, q{这是假设，不是知识——我明确标注它是假设。}, q{The sentence principal engineers remember. Label your guesses and your label becomes a skill.}],
['Close', q{Not to change your assessment — to close the loop properly.}, q{不为改变评价——为把闭环走完。}, q{The follow-up offer, disarmed: motivation stated as professionalism, not as damage control.}],
],
vocab => [
['Bluff', 'v. 硬编/虚张', 'Pretending knowledge you lack — the fastest way to fail an interview.'],
['Baseline', 'n. 基线', 'The measured before-state that makes any after-state meaningful.'],
['Staging run', 'n. 预发演练', 'A rehearsal in a production-like environment — where tuning belongs.'],
['Allocation rate', 'n. 分配率', 'How fast new objects are created; the driver of GC pressure.'],
['Pause time', 'n. 暂停时间', 'How long GC stops the world; the number batch jobs feel.'],
['Canonical write-up', 'n. 权威文章', 'The few trusted references — as opposed to folklore.'],
['Behind a flag', 'phr. 功能开关保护', 'Shipping changes toggleable at runtime, with instant rollback.'],
['Generational hypothesis', 'n. 分代假设', 'Most objects die young — the design basis of most GCs.'],
['Allocation churn', 'n. 分配扰动', 'High-volume short-lived object creation; the batch job disease.'],
['Close the loop', 'phr. 闭环', 'Complete a commitment end-to-end — including after the room.'],
],
grammar => [
['"I would not pretend…" — the refusal grammar', q{<em>"I would not pretend the knobs transfer."</em> Conditional + negation does the job of three sentences: I know the temptation, I decline it, and I know why. The pattern scales: <em>"I would not guess at settings I cannot test."</em>, <em>"I would not quote a number I cannot defend."</em> Refusals with reasons read as senior; bare "I don\'t know" reads as an ending, not a pivot.}, q{"I would not pretend the knobs transfer."——条件式 + 否定一句顶三句：我知道诱惑是什么、我拒绝它、我知道为什么。句式可复制："I would not guess at settings I cannot test."、"I would not quote a number I cannot defend."。带理由的拒绝显得资深；光秃秃的 "I don\'t know" 是句号，不是转折。}],
['Marking epistemic status', q{<em>"That is a hypothesis, not knowledge — I am marking it as such."</em> The grammar of intellectual honesty is a <strong>label pair</strong>: X, not Y. "A baseline, not a fix." "Adjacent knowledge, not expertise." One dash, one contrast, and everything after it can be trusted because you proved you grade your own claims.}, q{"That is a hypothesis, not knowledge — I am marking it as such."——知识诚实感来自<strong>标签对</strong>：X, not Y。"A baseline, not a fix."、"Adjacent knowledge, not expertise." 一个破折号一个对比，之后你说的话都更可信，因为你证明了自己会给自己的断言评级。}],
],
tips => [
q{<strong>Every interviewer plants an unanswerable question.</strong> Recognise it as the behavioural test it is — the answer is the method, not the fact.},
q{<strong>Never bluff in a bank interview.</strong> One caught bluff poisons ten true statements. Banks run on auditable claims; so should your interview.},
q{<strong>Adjacent knowledge is credit — if labelled.</strong> ".NET\'s generational hypothesis transfers" earns points; implying JVM experience does not.},
q{<strong>The follow-up email converts a miss into a story.</strong> 24 hours later: "I looked into JVM GC tuning as discussed — here is what I would try first, and why." Interviewers quote these.},
q{<strong>"I don\'t know, here\'s how I\'d find out" is a complete sentence.</strong> Practise it aloud until the pivot is reflex, not recovery.},
],
practice => [
{ t => 'The four-move drill', p => 'Pick a technology you genuinely do not know. Script your four moves: boundary (1 line), method (4 numbered steps), adjacent knowledge (2 items, labelled), follow-up offer (1 line).', a => q{Run it aloud with a friend playing Ruth. The boundary line must contain no apology and no excuse. The method must be specific enough that a stranger could execute it — "I would read the docs" is not a method.} },
{ t => 'Label pair factory', p => 'Write label pairs for: a rough estimate, a vendor claim, an anecdote from an old project.', a => q{"A first-pass estimate, not a commitment." "A vendor benchmark, not our workload." "An anecdote from 2021, not current behaviour." Each pair buys you honesty now and credibility later — the grammar of auditable speech.} },
{ t => 'Bluff detection', p => 'Which response fails hardest: (a) "GC tuning is basically the same everywhere, I\'d tweak the flags." (b) "I don\'t know." (c) "I\'ve never tuned a JVM — here\'s my Wednesday plan."', a => q{(a) fails hardest — it is a bluff a principal engineer detects in one follow-up, and it poisons the true things you said earlier. (b) is incomplete but honest. (c) is the professional answer: boundary, then method, then the loop.} },
],
