grammar => [
['Present perfect in anchors', q{<em>"Tomas mentioned…"</em> (past simple — the event) versus <em>"I\'ve noticed…"</em> (present perfect — knowledge you carry now). Anchoring questions use both: the mention is history, the noticing is current. <em>"I\'ve noticed the QA engineers rotate — what does that give you?"</em> The have-noticed frame marks the observation as yours, gathered, not googled.}, q{"Tomas mentioned…"（一般过去时——事件）对 "I\'ve noticed…"（现在完成时——你现在携带的认知）。锚定式提问两者都用：提及是历史，注意是当下。"I\'ve noticed the QA engineers rotate — what does that give you?"——have noticed 的框架表明观察是你自己收集的，不是搜来的。}],
['Future-in-the-past for the success question', q{<em>"Twelve months from now, this role has worked out. What specifically has happened?"</em> The grammar trick: speak from a future vantage point using present perfect — "has worked out", "has happened". It forces concrete, completed outcomes rather than vague intentions, which is exactly why the answer Grace gives is scoreable: three events, one bet.}, q{语法技巧：从未来的视角用现在完成时说话——"has worked out"、"has happened"。它逼迫对方给出具体的、已完成的成果而非模糊愿景——这正是 Grace 的回答可评分的原因：三个事件、一个下注。}],
],
